BLOCK|h1|1|Cluster-lang: Chapter 4 — Memory Safety & Ownership
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang provides strict compile-time safety checks resembling Rust, but with a highly simplified memory management model that hides lifetime complexity.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Non-Nullability by Default
BLOCK|p||In Cluster-lang, variables are non-nullable by default. They must be initialized with a value and cannot be assigned `None` or `nullptr`:
BLOCK|code|python|name: string = "Alice"      // Valid
name = None                 // Compile-time Type Mismatch Error!
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Optional Types (`?`)
BLOCK|p||If you want a variable to be nullable, append a `?` suffix to its type. Optional variables can be initialized or assigned `None`:
BLOCK|code|python|email: string? := None      // Valid optional string
email = "alice@example.com" // Assign a value
email = None                // Can be set back to None
BLOCK|p||Optional variables can be unwrapped using optional chaining (`?.`) or fallback using null coalescing (`??`).
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Box Pointers (`box[T]`)
BLOCK|p||A `box[T]` is a heap-allocated unique pointer that owns the underlying resource `T`.
BLOCK|list||Only **one** variable can own a box pointer at a time.
BLOCK|list||When the owner goes out of scope, the heap resource is automatically deleted (zero memory leaks).
BLOCK|code|python|x := box 42                 // Heap-allocated unique integer box
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. Ownership Transfer & Move Semantics (`move()`)
BLOCK|p||To transfer ownership of a resource (like a `box[T]` pointer or other custom resource), you must explicitly use the `move()` function:
BLOCK|code|python|val1 := box 100
val2 := move(val1)          // Ownership transferred to val2
BLOCK|h3|3|Compile-Time Use-After-Move Protection
BLOCK|p||If you try to access a variable after its ownership has been transferred, the Cluster compiler throws a compile-time safety error:
BLOCK|code|python|val1 := box 100
val2 := move(val1)
put *val1                  // Compile Error: Use of moved variable 'val1'
BLOCK|p||This guarantees memory safety and prevents double-free errors.
BLOCK|hr||
